Reason Behind less sucess rate in 2012: 

    Severe Recession: GDP contracted around –3%, continuing the post-2011 bailout downturn.

    EU–IMF Bailout Enforcement: Portugal operated under strict austerity after the 78 billion euro rescue in 2011.

    Austerity Measures: Government cut public spending, increased taxes, froze public sector wages, and reduced pensions.

    High Unemployment: Overall >15%, youth unemployment >30%, consumption severely constrained.

    Fiscal Pressure: Budget deficits forced strict control on public investment and social programs.

    Weak Domestic Demand: Household spending plummeted due to layoffs and rising taxes.

    Exports Slightly Stabilized Economy: Tourism and manufactured exports provided minor relief.

    Financial Market Strain: Sovereign bond yields remained high; borrowing costs elevated.

    No Significant Monthly Shocks: Economic trends were continuous; May had no unique impact.